### 2. Performance Optimization

Performance optimization improves the speed and efficiency of Power BI reports and dashboards. Slow reports can affect user 
experience and increase loading time. Optimization techniques reduce memory usage and improve query performance. It helps
increase responsiveness and minimizes unnecessary processing. Large datasets and complex calculations can negatively impact
report performance. Proper optimization improves system stability and creates a better user experience. Organizations 
apply optimization techniques to ensure reports load faster and work smoothly even with large amounts of data.

**Methods of Optimization:**

* Remove unnecessary columns
* Reduce dataset size
* Use star schema design
* Avoid complex calculations
* Use aggregations
* Reduce visual count
* Optimize DAX formulas

**Example:**
Instead of creating multiple calculated columns, use measures because they consume less memory.

---

### 3. Query Folding

Query Folding is the process where Power Query sends data transformation steps back to the source database instead of
processing them locally. This reduces processing load and improves refresh performance. Not all transformations 
support query folding. Power BI tries to push operations like filtering and sorting to the source system. Query 
folding reduces memory consumption and improves refresh efficiency. It helps process large amounts of data faster by
using the power of database servers rather than Power BI resources.

**Advantages:**

* Faster refresh
* Reduced memory usage
* Better performance
* Less processing in Power BI

**Example:**

```SQL
SELECT * FROM Sales
WHERE Amount > 1000
```

---

### 4. Incremental Refresh

Incremental Refresh updates only newly added or modified data instead of refreshing the entire dataset. This significantly
reduces refresh time for large datasets. It is commonly used in large business systems where data changes frequently.
Incremental refresh improves system efficiency because old records are not repeatedly processed. It minimizes server 
workload and reduces system resource consumption. Organizations use this feature for handling continuously growing 
datasets and maintaining updated business reports.

**Benefits:**

* Faster refresh
* Lower resource usage
* Better performance
* Reduced refresh failures

**Example:**
If a database contains 10 years of sales data, Power BI refreshes only the last month's records.

---

### 5. Composite Models

Composite Models allow combining different storage modes and data sources in one model. Users can mix Import mode and
DirectQuery mode together. This provides flexibility and supports large-scale analytics. Composite models allow
businesses to work with both historical and real-time data. They improve scalability and provide efficient management 
of multiple data sources. Organizations can build more dynamic reporting systems using composite models.

**Storage Modes:**

* Import
* DirectQuery
* Dual Mode

**Example:**
Sales data is imported while live customer data uses DirectQuery.

---

### 6. Aggregations

Aggregations improve performance by storing summarized data instead of detailed records. Power BI queries summarized 
tables first, reducing processing time. Aggregations are useful for handling large datasets. They reduce the number of
calculations required and improve report loading speed. Aggregations help reduce resource consumption and improve 
overall report responsiveness. Organizations use aggregation techniques for faster reporting and better business 
intelligence solutions.

**Types of Aggregations:**

* Sum
* Count
* Average
* Minimum
* Maximum

**Example:**
Instead of storing every sales transaction, monthly sales totals are stored.

